---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { ORIGINAL_SITE_URL } from "../consts";

const languages = ["C", "C++", "C#", "Java"];
const speedFacts = [
  { label: "Compression speed", value: "2–8 MB/s on a 4 GHz CPU (2 threads)" },
  { label: "Decompression speed", value: "30–100 MB/s on a 4 GHz CPU, or 5–15 MB/s on a 1 GHz RISC CPU" },
  { label: "Decompression memory", value: "8–32 KB, plus the dictionary size" },
  { label: "Decompression code size", value: "2–8 KB" },
];
---

<Layout
  title="LZMA SDK"
  description="The LZMA SDK: public-domain documentation, samples, and libraries for building on LZMA compression, in C, C++, C#, and Java."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">SDK · v26.02</p>
    <h1>LZMA SDK</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The documentation, samples, header files, libraries, and tools you need
      to develop applications that use LZMA compression — the same method
      that powers the 7z format by default.
    </p>
    <div class="button-row">
      <a class="button primary" href="https://github.com/ip7z/7zip/releases/download/26.02/lzma2602.7z">
        Download LZMA SDK <span class="sub">26.02 · 1.7 MB</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel highlight">
    <h2>Public domain</h2>
    <p>
      As of version 4.62, the LZMA SDK is public domain: "Anyone is free to
      copy, modify, publish, use, compile, sell, or distribute the original
      LZMA SDK code, either in source code form or as a compiled binary, for
      any purpose, commercial or non-commercial, and by any means."
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What's inside</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Languages</h3>
        <ul>
          {languages.map((l) => <li>{l}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p>Windows binaries ship for x86, x64, and arm64.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Source &amp; examples</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>C++ LZMA encoder/decoder source</li>
          <li>C++ .7z compression/decompression (reduced)</li>
          <li>ANSI-C source for LZMA / LZMA2 / XZ</li>
          <li>ANSI-C 7z decompression example</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Tools</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><code>lzma.exe</code> — standalone compressor</li>
          <li><code>7zr.exe</code> — reduced 7-Zip</li>
          <li>SFX modules for self-extracting archives and installers</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Where it's used</h3>
        <p>
          Suitable for embedded applications thanks to a small decompression
          footprint — ROM and firmware compression are common uses, alongside
          general-purpose archiving.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Speed and footprint</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Metric</th>
          <th>Figure</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {speedFacts.map((f) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{f.label}</td>
            <td>{f.value}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Where the name comes from</h2>
    <p>
      LZMA originally stood for Lempel-Ziv-Markov chain-Arithmetic coding. In
      the final version, arithmetic coding was replaced by range coding — the
      name stuck anyway.
    </p>
    <p>See the real page on <a href={`${ORIGINAL_SITE_URL}sdk.html`}>7-zip.org/sdk.html</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Layout>
